Freddie Kitchens"},{"season":2022,"team":"TEN","distance":0.5925,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"WAS","distance":0.6126,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"NO","distance":0.6372,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Pete Carmichael"}],"defense":[{"season":2023,"team":"DEN","distance":0.6048,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vance Joseph"},{"season":2022,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6656,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2019,"team":"TB","distance":0.6938,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Todd Bowles"},{"season":2024,"team":"DAL","distance":0.7235,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"ATL","distance":0.729,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Jeff Ulbrich"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Jets went 4-12 in 2018 and were outscored 441 to 333. The defense did most of the work, finishing 22nd in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 30th in EPA per play. What set them apart was the 3rd highest 13 personnel usage and a blitz rate that was among the highest in the league.","offense":"The Jets offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd highest 13 personnel usage and the 3rd lowest 22 personnel usage in the league. Sam Darnold ran the passing game, with Robbie Chosen leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Jets defense was a little below average by EPA allowed per play. Even its strongest area, pressure rate, only ranked 14th, and it was 24th in sack rate. Henry Anderson led the team with 7 sacks, and Jordan Jenkins added 7 despite playing only 59% of the snaps. Schematically, they had a blitz rate that was among the highest in the league.","special":"The Jets were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 92% of field goals, which was one of the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 23% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 44% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Sam Darnold played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,865 yards. Targets were spread around, with Robbie Chosen and Jermaine Kearse leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Isaiah Crowell and Elijah McGuire. On defense, Henry Anderson led the team with 7 sacks and Trumaine Johnson picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Cardinals under Drew Petzing and the 2018 Browns under Freddie Kitchens. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Broncos under Vance Joseph and the 2022 Chargers under Brandon Staley.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Jets changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}